//? Title: NumPy Interoperability: Layout and Byte Order
//? Subtitle: Cross-language agreement includes layout, byte order, dtype and shape
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
NPY files describe logical coordinates independently of payload layout and byte order.
Native C++ writes Fortran-order and big-endian variants, checks their decoded coordinates,
and exports a transposed array. The regression suite also retains independently NumPy-written
fixtures; the executable report requires no Python data-preparation step.
)
\toc

> The method

An NPY header records a dtype descriptor, a Fortran-order flag and a shape tuple; the payload
contains contiguous elements in that declared order. For a two by three matrix, the address
rules differ:

$$
\mathrm{Coffset}(i,j)=3i+j,\qquad \mathrm{Foffset}(i,j)=i+2j.
$$
\alttext(C order advances columns first, while Fortran order advances rows first.)

Endianness is independent of this index mapping. The native reader converts layout into logical
tensor coordinates and swaps multibyte values when required. NPZ is a ZIP archive of named NPY
members; stored or deflated members are accepted. Native writes default to little-endian C-order NPY
version 1.0; explicit writer options select Fortran order and big-endian bytes. Object arrays are unsupported: no pickle is loaded. Both the reader and writer copy
data, so this is file interchange rather than zero-copy shared memory. Work is O(payload bytes).

The concept diagram has a retained TikZ source in `diagrams/numpy_interop_concept.tikz`.
Regeneration requires LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, and dvisvgm.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/numpy_interop_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/numpy_interop_concept.tikz > build/numpy_interop_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/numpy_interop_concept.svg build/numpy_interop_concept.dvi > build/numpy_interop_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered build/numpy_interop_concept.svg from local TikZ source.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered build/numpy_interop_concept.svg from local TikZ source.
// \image(figures/numpy_interop_concept.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Cross-language agreement includes layout, byte order, dtype and shape)
\alttext(Native NPY writer; Fortran and big-endian storage Then Logical coordinate checks Then Native transpose export; specified coordinates verified.)

> Simple examples

```{cpp, results="output", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/nn/array_io.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
 const auto x=n::arange(0.,6.,1.,n::DType::Float64).reshape({2,3});
 n::save_npy("build/numpy_interop_fortran.npy",x,true,false);
 n::save_npy("build/numpy_interop_big.npy",x.to(n::DType::Int32),false,true);
 std::print("Native format variants: [[0,1,2],[3,4,5]], Fortran Float64 and big-endian Int32.\n");
}
```
// result_begin:
// Native format variants: [[0,1,2],[3,4,5]], Fortran Float64 and big-endian Int32.
// result_end

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/numpy_interop_values.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;const auto f=n::load_npy("build/numpy_interop_fortran.npy"),b=n::load_npy("build/numpy_interop_big.npy");
 if(f.shape()!=n::Shape({2,3})||f.to_vector<int>()!=std::vector<int>({0,1,2,3,4,5})||f.to_vector<int>()!=b.to_vector<int>())return 1;
 n::save_npy("build/numpy_interop_cpp.npy",n::transpose(f));
 auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();p.lines({0,1,2,3,4,5},f.to_vector<double>(),"logical values")
 .title("Logical values survive a layout change").x_label("logical flattened index").y_label("value");
 p.size(820,420).save_svg("build/numpy_interop_values.svg");std::print("Fortran shape {}; big-endian dtype {}; C++ transpose saved as [3,2].\n",n::shape_str(f.shape()),n::dtype_name(b.dtype()));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Fortran shape [2, 3]; big-endian dtype int32; C++ transpose saved as [3,2].
\image(figures/numpy_interop_values.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Layout and byte order change storage, while the decoded logical values remain the same.)
\alttext(Layout and byte order change storage, while the decoded logical values remain the same.)

> Checking the theory

```{cpp, results="output", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/nn/array_io.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;
 const auto x=n::load_npy("build/numpy_interop_cpp.npy");
 if(x.shape()!=n::Shape{3,2}||x.dtype()!=n::DType::Float64||x.to_vector<double>()!=std::vector<double>{0,3,1,4,2,5})return 1;
 std::print("All six specified transposed coordinates, Float64 dtype and [3,2] shape verified.\n");
}
```
// result_begin:
// All six specified transposed coordinates, Float64 dtype and [3,2] shape verified.
// result_end

> Conclusion

The native runs agree with all six specified coordinates in each layout. Independent NumPy-written reader fixtures remain in the regression suite. Header metadata is as
important as payload bytes. This workflow checks numeric file interoperability; it does not
provide shared memory or load arbitrary NumPy object arrays.

> References

- [NumPy NPY format specification](https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/generated/numpy.lib.format.html).
